"""Transparency cards.

Design notes
------------
*The card is generated by the engine, not by the model.* Card text is a pure
function of the rated signals, so two runs on identical packets print identical
cards, and a card can never claim a number the log does not contain.

*Numbers are optional.* ``skill.numeric_cards: false`` suppresses every figure.
This matters for Study 2: printing "0.68 / 0.55" invites participants to rate the
number rather than the behaviour, and it makes the treatment partly a
quantified-self interface. The variant is a legitimate experimental factor, so it
is a config flag rather than a code edit.

*Disagreement is more visible than the number.* A participant who sees one
match is not being told that 0.57 could equally have been 0.36 under slightly
different rating. ``show_disagreement_band`` widens the alert option by a
tolerance and prints it, so the reader can see the decision boundary is soft.

*The branch is always labelled.* A dissonance-reduction card says so on its face.
Without that label a denial strategy reads as the system endorsing source
discounting, which would be a real harm and not merely a measurement problem.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Any

# How far a rating may move before the same packet could plausibly land on the
# other side of an alert threshold. Reported, never hidden.
#
# This is a *ceiling on what counts as tight*, not the number printed on a card.
# It was a hard-coded printed constant in v0.2.0, which made the card assert a
# packet-relative fact that was false for most packets - the shipped example had
# 0.16 of room and the card claimed 0.06. The printed value is now computed from
# the packet by ``index_margin``; this constant only decides when the card
# escalates its wording, and equals the config default for
# ``transparency.tight_margin``.
RATING_TOLERANCE_CEILING = 0.06

